            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls &amp; the weather stripping along the exterior doors. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed.  

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The major area of air leakage &amp; infiltration was observed coming from the crawlspace. I suspect this is main issue which has been making the lower floor feel cool, as well as cooling down the ducting (which passes through the crawlspace) making it difficult for heat to travel towards the entry area register. Please see foundation section for the main cause and potential options. 

There was an open joist space found within the crawlpaces ceiling, which the plumbing for the secondary suite travels through. This open joinst space was located along the back, underneath the bathroom/kitchen dividing wall. The joist spaces should be insulated, and sealed. As the crawlspace has a lot of air leakage in general, I suspect the temperatures down there have gotten quite low during the peak winter months, likely causing the frozen water lines you've experienced. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 160 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for air sealing improvements from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. 

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional. 

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>

CEILING INSULATION



The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R28 of insulation, comprised of blown cellulose insulation. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R60. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R28 up to a minimum R60, this is estimated to save $140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.6 GJ, and qualify for a $ 125 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. Please note, at the time of writing (December 2021) the EEIP rebate is fully subscribed and Arctic Energy Alliance is currently taking a waitlist. Check in with AEA after April 1st, 2022 to see if circumstances have changed.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



There are also rebates available for attic insulation through the Greener Homes Grant, offered by the Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

Due to the way in which the foundation was designed with the home, there is an exposed ceiling located in the crawlspace area. This exposed area is actually the concrete section located outside of your front door (about 4 ft x 4 ft). This ceiling area of your crawlspace appears to have no insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation. As this area uses a metal sheet support (on which the conrete was poured), frost is likely easily attracted here, along with some moisture. Ensure you use a type of insulation which does not obsorb moisture (for exmaple, fiberglass batt acts like a sponge with moisture, while rigid extruded polystyrene does not abosorb moisture). 



Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation to the underside of this crawlspace ceiling area, you could potentially save $ 50 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ.



If you are interested in adding additional insulation to your ceiling, please contact AEA to discuss how we can help and to learn about the incentives available. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for increasing home insulation in attics, cathedral ceilings and flat roofs. The amount you are eligible for will depend on factors such as what insulation is currently in your home, how much insulation you add and type of attic/ceiling your home has. Insulation slows the rate of heat loss, resulting in improved energy use and can help save money. Installing insulation in an attic, cathedral ceiling or flat roof is eligible for a grant, as long as minimum levels of insulation and coverage are achieved. If your house consists of more than one roof or roof type, the grant amount will be pro-rated or calculated based on roof type and area.

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 8.0 inches of concrete, with 1 inch of polyiso (R5.5). I have modelled increasing this insulation up to a total of R20 worth of insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R20 insulation on the crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 70 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. 



If you are interested in adding additional insulation to your crawlspace walls, please contact AEA to discuss how we can help and to learn about the incentives available. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area, and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $1300 is available for adding more than RSI 3.87 (R-22) insulation to crawl space exterior walls and headers. Insulating crawl space walls and headers can reduce energy costs and improve comfort in the occupied space above the crawl space. The grant is available if insulation is added to all exterior crawl space walls and headers. A grant is available for adding a minimum of RSI 1.76 (R-10) insulation.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CRAWLSPACE AIR LEAKAGE

Due to the way in which the foundation was designed along with the home design, the front deck (outside the tenants bedroom window) is actually visible from the crawlspace, and is not insulated or fully sealed. This is the main area a ton of air leakage is easily infiltrating into the homes foundation. Currently, there is a 2x4 studded wall, with only an inch of insulation dividing this area from the crawlspace, however has a gap of roughly 6 inches from the ground for moisture control. 



With this area, we have two potential solutions. 

Option A, encapsolating this space to truely bring it into the heated space of the crawlspace. In order to accomplish this, the concrete walls should be insulated, as well as the underside of the deck including an air barrier. Precautions should be introduced for weather protection from above, as snow/rain can easily accumulate overtop. 



Option B, redesigning the current 2x4 studded wall to create a better air seal from this area underneath the deck. This involves properly air sealing the wall, and adding additional insulation between the studs. One important thing to note with this option;once the dividing wall has been sealed, the area underneath the deck would no longer have a direct path for moisture control and ventilation, potentially introducing moisture related problems such as mould. A ventilation exhaust fan system deticated to this small enclosed area should be considered if option B is selected. </foundation>
                <windows>

Your windows are triple pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in good shape, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, as you mentioned this area is paticularily cold (and I suspect the convective cooling from the windows is the culprit). In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 

*As mentioned, your windows are in good shape, and likely not worth considering window replacements unless nessesary. 



Savings: 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 200 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.2 GJ.   There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 287 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save $ 1,400 per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil-fired furnace was originally rated at 82.1% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 97% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,200 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights Â Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

 

        - two rangehood fans

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $0.73 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



